# AsyncIO Provenance Record: Martin Taillefer Permission

The following email correspondence establishes the explicit Public Domain
dedication of the core AsyncIO routines by the original author,
Martin Taillefer, to me for use in the SDMake project and downstream
distribution.

My follow up question was made before i realized the file-dates
on asyncio.c and asyncio.h in NDK_3.1 matched the file date on asyncio.doc in
NDK_3.5. And that the latest date in the changelog in the latter was only one
day later than said file-dates. And it seems the changes described in the
changelog are included in the version in NDK_3.1.

---

From: Me <address redacted>
Date: Fri, Jun 12, 2026 at 1:48 PM
To: Martin Taillefer

Hi.
I have the latest years been trying to fulfill my childhood dream of rally
learning C on Amiga using SAS/C.
At the moment I'm working on fork of dmake from the now BSD-3-Clause licensed
DICE, with backports from dxmake from DragonFly BSD, for replacing smake.
My project is GPL, and when I was trying to fix the Readme to make sure I was
compliant with this mix, I realize I had made the mistake of assuming that
AsyncIO.lha on Aminet was "Public Domain".
I have imported it to my repo, stating it keeps it original license, fixed one
bug and made some changes to make it work even with AmigaOS 1.3 for my use case.
But I realize I did not have permission to do that. (I must add I tried to do
the right thing, and made my changes to tose files Public Domain.)
I tried the newest mail address of Magnus Holmgren I could find, but that did
not exist.
So since the .guide states that I got his permissions from you, and the fact you
are the original author, I must ask.
Can you please give me permission to use this code under a license that make
what I have done legal?
My project is located here: https://github.com/Hagbard-Celin/SDMake/tree/master

signature redacted

---

From: Me <address redacted>
Date: Fri, Jun 12, 2026 at 3:15 PM
To: Martin Taillefer

After thinking this through some more. (Sorry for not doing that first and
keeping it all in one mail.)
I should have phrased my question:
Can you please give me permission to use the parts of this code that you wrote
under a license that make what I have done legal?

And in the meantime I think I have managed to track down current e-mail
addresses for Magnus Holmgren and Michael B. Smith.
So if your answer is affirmative, I can ask for their permission for their
respective contributions under the license you choose.

signature redacted

---

From: Martin Taillefer
Date: Fri, Jun 12, 2026 at 3:29 PM
To: Me

Hi there, Of course, please consider the code public domain. Use, modify, and
distribute in any way you'd like.

---

From: Me <address redacted>
Date: Tue, Jun 16, 2026 at 6:31 PM
To: Martin Taillefer

Hi, sorry for bothering you again.
But, while trying to reconstruct the history of the AsyncIO code, I discovered
that some of the fixes from your later versions where never integrated into the
version on Aminet.
Since my previous question referred to the Aminet archive when I asked about the
parts of the code you wrote, my understanding is that to incorporate those fixes
in my repo, I'm legally required to ask:
Can you please confirm if I can consider all versions of the AsyncIO code for
AmigaDOS written by you public domain.

signature redacted

---

From: Martin Taillefer
Date: Tue, Jun 16, 2026 at 7:02 PM
To: Me

Yes, consider all versions, editions, corrections, as being public domain.
